# Space Race - ComS 4370 Project 1B
# Author - Dylan Topic - Dylan004@iastate.edu
3D space racing game made with MonoGame and the Bepu Physics V2 engine for 
ComS 4370. Fly a ship through a sequence of rings as fast as you can with 
as few misses as possible.

---

## How to run

Make sure you have .NET 8 (or newer) installed, then just run:

    dotnet run

---

## Controls

- W/S - pitch nose down/up
- A/D - yaw left/right  
- Q/E - roll left/right
- Left Shift - thrust forward
- Left Ctrl - reverse thrust
- R - restart the run
- Escape - exit

---

## How to play

Fly through the cyan-highlighted ring. After you pass it (or miss it), the 
next ring lights up. Get through all 7 rings as fast as you can while 
missing as few as possible. The HUD shows your current time, speed, ring 
count, and pass/miss totals. When you finish the course the clock freezes 
and a final score is shown.

---

## Project structure

    SpaceRace/
     Content/
       Hud.spritefont        - font description for the HUD
       Content.mgcb          - content pipeline build file
     Physics/
       NarrowPhaseCallbacks.cs   - Bepu callback for collision filtering
       PoseIntegratorCallbacks.cs - Bepu callback for gravity and damping
       PhysicsObject.cs          - bridges a Bepu body to a MonoGame transform
     Rendering/
       Camera.cs             - chase camera with frame rate independent smoothing
       ShipPrimitive.cs      - procedural ship mesh (fuselage, nose, wings, fin)
       TorusPrimitive.cs     - procedural torus mesh used for rings
       Skybox.cs             - 6-face procedural starfield skybox
     Gameplay/
       Ship.cs               - ship class, applies forces from keyboard input
       Ring.cs               - one ring with its Bepu static body and visual state
       CourseManager.cs      - ring sequence, pass/miss detection, clock
     UI/
       Hud.cs                - HUD rendering and score formula
     Game1.cs                  - main game loop with fixed step physics
     Program.cs                - entry point

---

## The course

The course has 7 rings placed along a path in -Z with varying offsets and 
orientations. Some rings are tilted left/right, some pitched up/down, and 
one is rolled diagonally. Each ring is:

- a procedural torus mesh (major radius 10, minor radius 0.6)
- a Bepu static body (infinite mass, cant be pushed)
- soft white when not yet reached
- pulsing cyan when its the next target
- dim gray after you pass or miss it

The skybox is a unit cube wrapped around the camera with 6 procedurally 
generated starfield textures, drawn first each frame with depth writes off 
so world geometry overdraws naturally. The cube follows the camera so stars 
appear infinitely far.

---

## Physics

All ship motion goes through the Bepu V2 physics engine. The simulation 
runs at a fixed 60 Hz with an accumulator so behavior is the same on any 
monitor refresh rate. Here is what the physics layer does:

- zero gravity (its space)
- mild linear and angular damping so motion still bleeds off over time
- keyboard input is converted to forces and torques in the ships local 
  frame (forward is -Z), then applied as impulses each fixed step
- auto stabilizer applies a counter-torque on any rotation axis that isnt 
  receiving input, so the ship settles when you let go of the keys (this 
  is a standard steering behavior, still forces only)
- rings are registered with Bepu as static bodies so they have infinite 
  mass per the assignment
- a narrow phase callback filters out static-vs-dynamic contacts so the 
  ship doesnt physically bump the ring colliders
- pass/miss detection is analytic: each frame the ships previous and 
  current positions are checked against the target rings plane, and if 
  they straddle it the crossing point is interpolated and checked against 
  the ring radius

---

## HUD and scoring

The HUD shows:

- time elapsed (freezes on course complete)
- current speed in m/s
- ring count (0/7 through 7/7)
- passed and missed totals (color shifts to salmon if any misses)
- final score overlay when the course is complete

The score formula is:

    score = max(0, 10000 - (time * 10) - (missed * 500))
    if missed == 0 then score += 1000  (clean run bonus)

Each missed ring costs roughly 50 seconds of equivalent time, so accuracy 
matters more than raw speed. A perfect ~60 second run scores around 10400.

---

## Notes

- The ship has a 2x1x3 box collider registered with Bepu, but the narrow phase callback 
  filters out all static-vs-dynamic contacts, so in practice the ship doesnt physically 
  collide with anything. Pass/miss is detected analytically by the CourseManager. 
  The collision box is essentially reserved for future obstacles
- The rings static bodies have a tiny sphere shape just to satisfy "ring is 
  a Bepu body with infinite mass." Actual pass/miss is detected by the 
  CourseManager not by Bepu collisions
- The starfield textures use a fixed RNG seed so the skybox looks the same 
  every run

